2.6 "Authorised Personnel" means individuals who hold a valid Port Access Badge issued by the Port Security Unit, or who are included on a valid crew list or vessel manifest.

3.0 GENERAL ACCESS ELIGIBILITY

3.1 Access to the Port Area is restricted. Access eligibility is determined by the Lagos Port Authority, and all persons, vehicles, and cargo seeking entry must satisfy the criteria set out in this Directive.

3.2 The following categories of persons are eligible to apply for access:

a) Employees of Terminal Operators, Shipping Lines, and licensed agents, whose duties require them to be present within the Port Area;

b) Employees of the Lagos Port Authority and of the Nigerian Ports Authority;

c) Employees of other Federal Government agencies with statutory functions within the Port Area, including but not limited to the Nigeria Customs Service, the Nigeria Immigration Service, the Nigeria Police Force, the Department of State Services, NIMASA, and the Port Health Services;

d) Licensed stevedores and dockworkers registered with the relevant joint industrial council, when engaged in authorised operations;

e) Truck drivers and their assistants, when attending the Port Area for the purpose of collecting or delivering cargo under a valid gate pass or booking;

f) Visiting officials, auditors, and inspectors, when accompanied by an authorised Port Authority official or upon production of a valid letter of introduction;

g) Members of the public, only when visiting the Port Area for an approved purpose, such as attendance at a scheduled meeting, official inquiry, or guided tour.

3.3 Access eligibility does not guarantee entry. Entry may be refused where the purpose of the visit is not established, where the visitor cannot produce the required documentation, or where the Port Security Unit determines that entry would compromise security or safety.

4.0 APPLICATION PROCEDURES

