b) Any security incidents or threats reported during the voyage;

c) The names and nationalities of all persons on board, including crew, passengers, and any stowaways or unauthorized persons discovered;

d) A list of all cargo on board, with particular attention to dangerous goods, hazardous materials, and any items of a sensitive or controlled nature; and

e) The vessel's last ten (10) ports of call and any changes to the itinerary notified after the initial Vessel Arrival Notification.

3.3 Failure to submit the Vessel Arrival Notification or the Pre-Arrival Security Information within the stipulated timeframes may result in the vessel being directed to anchor at a designated anchorage pending the submission of the required information. No claim for demurrage or other compensation shall lie against the Authority in such circumstances.

4. APPLICATION FOR HARBOR MASTER APPROVAL FOR BERTHING (HMAB)

4.1 The Agent shall submit an application for HMAB to the Harbor Master's Office not later than forty-eight (48) hours before the ETA. The application shall be in the form set out in Appendix B to this Directive and shall be accompanied by:

a) A copy of the Berth Request Form submitted to the Scheduling Office;

b) A copy of the Vessel Arrival Notification and all supporting documents submitted under Section 3;

c) A copy of the vessel's Safety Management Certificate and the company's Document of Compliance issued under the International Safety Management (ISM) Code;

d) A copy of the vessel's crew list and the certificates of competency of the Master and the Chief Engineer;

e) A certificate of insurance covering the vessel's liabilities for damage to port property, pollution, and third-party claims, in a sum not less than the amount specified in the Port Tariff;

f) A letter of undertaking from the Agent or the vessel's owner confirming that all port dues, charges, and fees will be paid in accordance with the Port Tariff before the vessel sails; and
